The watermarked text is detected through a one-sided $z$ -test by testing the null hypothesis where the text is not watermarked. The $z$ -score is calculated using the number of recognized green tokens in the text. Then, the testing text is considered as watermarked if the $z$ -score is greater than $z_{\mathrm{threshold}}$ . Note that the detection algorithm with the higher $z_{\mathrm{threshold}}$ can result the lower false positive rate (FPR) and reduce Type I errors.

Spike Entropy Kirchenbauer et al. (2023a) used spike entropy for measuring how spread out a distribution is. Given a token probability vector $p$ and a scalar $m$ , spike entropy of $p$ with modulus $m$ is defined as:

